second artist, birth date for a different person, or second sport is evidence to retain it. Check each proposed deletion once more against all originals before producing the final short list.\nInput: {\"question\": \"When was Cove Fort built?\", \"slot_hint\": \"other\", \"all_original_sources\": {\"D1S1\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 82, \"text\": \"Cove Fort Cove Fort is a fort and historical site located in Millard County, Utah.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00\", \"start\": 83, \"end\": 160, \"text\": \"It was founded in 1867 by Ira Hinckley (the paternal grandfather of Gordon B.\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00\", \"start\": 161, \"end\": 203, \"text\": \"Hinckley) at the request of Brigham Young.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00\", \"start\": 204, \"end\": 363, \"text\": \"One of its distinctive features is the use of volcanic rock in the construction of the walls, rather than the wood used in many mid-19th-century western forts.\"}, \"D1S5\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00\", \"start\": 364, \"end\": 469, \"text\": \"This difference in construction is the reason it is one of very few forts of this period still surviving.\"}, \"D1S6\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00\", \"start\": 470, \"end\": 571, \"text\": \"Cove Fort is the closest named place to the western terminus of Interstate 70, resulting in Cove Fort\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 92, \"text\": \"families who lived there and about their efforts to provide for the needs of the travelers .\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01\", \"start\": 93, \"end\": 186, \"text\": \"Ira Hinckley , his brother Arza , and local workmen built the fort of volcanic rock in 1867 .\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01\", \"start\": 187, \"end\": 320, \"text\": \"They built it to protect themselves and travelers from possible Native American raids resulting from the Black Hawk War ( 1865–68 ) .\"}, \"D2S4\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01\", \"start\": 321, \"end\": 363, \"text\": \"Native Americans never attacked the fort .\"}, \"D2S5\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01\", \"start\": 364, \"end\": 500, \"text\": \"During the fort ’ s use as a way station , the Hinckley family maintained good relationships with local Pahvant Ute and Navajo Indians .\"}, \"D2S6\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01\", \"start\": 501, \"end\": 561, \"text\": \"Operating a self-sufficient fort was largely a family affair\"}, \"D3S1\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d02\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 148, \"text\": \"What to Expect When You Visit the Cove Fort Historic Site What to Expect When You Visit the Cove Fort Historic Site Cove Fort is now open for tours.\"}, \"D3S2\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d02\", \"start\": 149, \"end\": 301, \"text\": \"COVID 19 visitor information Updates Cove Fort Historic Site welcomes visitors year-round and is located approximately 30 miles south of Fillmore, Utah.\"}, \"D3S3\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d02\", \"start\": 302, \"end\": 443, \"text\": \"The rock fort was built in 1857 and protected telegraph lines and travelers on the road between Salt Lake City and settlements further south.\"}, \"D3S4\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d02\", \"start\": 444, \"end\": 606, \"text\": \"Leaders of The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints called families to manage the fort and ranch, and these families lived and worked there for many years.\"}, \"D4S1\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 91, \"text\": \"Families who lived there and about their efforts to provide for the needs of the travelers.\"}, \"D4S2\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03\", \"start\": 92, \"end\": 182, \"text\": \"Ira Hinckley, his brother Arza, and local workmen built the fort of volcanic rock in 1855.\"}, \"D4S3\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03\", \"start\": 183, \"end\": 313, \"text\": \"They built it to protect themselves and travelers from possible Native American raids resulting from the Black Hawk War (1865–68).\"}, \"D4S4\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03\", \"start\": 314, \"end\": 355, \"text\": \"Native Americans never attacked the fort.\"}, \"D4S5\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03\", \"start\": 356, \"end\": 488, \"text\": \"During the fort’s use as a way station, the Hinckley family maintained good relationships with local Pahvant Ute and Navajo Indians.\"}, \"D4S6\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03\", \"start\": 489, \"end\": 550, \"text\": \"Operating a self-sufficient fort was largely a family affair.\"}, \"D5S1\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d04\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 111, \"text\": \"Cove Fort, County Cork Cove Fort is a small bastioned land battery to the east of Cobh in County Cork, Ireland.\"}, \"D5S2\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d04\", \"start\": 112, \"end\": 306, \"text\": \"Built as a coastal defence fortification in 1743, on instruction of the then Vice-Admiral of the Coast, it replaced a number of temporary coastal artillery batteries which defended Cork Harbour.\"}, \"D5S3\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d04\", \"start\": 307, \"end\": 498, \"text\": \"The seaward fortifications included a demi-bastioned frontage with three tiers of gun emplacements commanding the harbour's main shipping channel and defending the naval yards at Haulbowline.\"}, \"D5S4\": {\"document_number\": 5, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d04\", \"start\": 499, \"end\": 657, \"text\": \"While the landward walls included musketry flanking-galleries, later 18th century reports criticised the fact that the fort was overlooked by higher ground to\"}}, \"original_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"Cove Fort\", \"scope\": \"construction\", \"claim\": \"Cove Fort was built in 1867.\", \"answer\": \"1867\", \"quote\": \"It was founded in 1867 by Ira Hinckley (the paternal grandfather of Gordon B.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"Cove Fort\", \"scope\": \"construction date\", \"claim\": \"Cove Fort was built in 1867.\", \"answer\": \"1867\", \"quote\": \"Ira Hinckley , his brother Arza , and local workmen built the fort of volcanic rock in 1867 .\", \"original_document_number\": 2}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d02\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d02:c0\", \"entity\": \"Cove Fort\", \"scope\": \"The rock fort\", \"claim\": \"The rock fort was built in 1857.\", \"answer\": \"1857\", \"quote\": \"The rock fort was built in 1857 and protected telegraph lines and travelers on the road between Salt Lake City and settlements further south.\", \"original_document_number\": 3}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03:c0\", \"entity\": \"Cove Fort\", \"scope\": \"construction\", \"claim\": \"Cove Fort was built in 1855.\", \"answer\": \"1855\", \"quote\": \"Ira Hinckley, his brother Arza, and local workmen built the fort of volcanic rock in 1855.\", \"original_document_number\": 4}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d04\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d04:c0\", \"entity\": \"Cove Fort\", \"scope\": \"construction date\", \"claim\": \"Cove Fort was built in 1743.\", \"answer\": \"1743\", \"quote\": \"Built as a coastal defence fortification in 1743, on instruction of the then Vice-Admiral of the Coast, it replaced a number of temporary coastal artillery batteries which defended Cork Harbour.\", \"original_document_number\": 5}], \"R5_answers\": [\"1867\", \"1857\", \"1855\", \"1743\"], \"candidates\": [{\"id\": \"C0\", \"answer\": \"1867\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C1\", \"answer\": \"1857\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C2\", \"answer\": \"1855\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C3\", \"answer\": \"1743\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}]}"
